<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conquest of Ceuta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1415</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> was the capture of the Muslim port on the North African coast by Portuguese forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">King João I</a> and his sons, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry</a>, in 1415. It initiated the overseas expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>. The city proved a commercial disappointment as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> bypassed it, encouraging Portuguese exploration along the African coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conquest-of-ceuta.jpg" alt="Conquest of Ceuta" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alvesgaspar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porto_April_2019-7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
